Original commit body from c04a7cf8e797af7c0ca516b31abfafeca5dbd217:

Both analyzers emitted the `enum` CONTAINER and none of its constants, so
the containment linker had nothing to root and a reverse slice from an
enum returned the container alone. Java additionally emitted an enum's
METHODS (`Color.label`), which is what made the gap easy to miss: an enum
with one method looks populated to any "does this container have a
member" probe while every constant is invisible.

Constants become kind="field" named `Color.RED`, matching each analyzer's
existing class-field naming, whose `.` the containment linker splits on.
Only the node types differ -- Java parses constants as `enum_constant`
under an `enum_body`, C# as `enum_member_declaration` under an
`enum_member_declaration_list`. Java reads only the DIRECT enum_constant
children, leaving the sibling `enum_body_declarations` (the enum's
methods and fields) to their own branches. A Java constant with arguments
(`RED("r")`) and a C# member with an explicit value (`Green = 2`) each
carry extra children after the identifier and are otherwise the same node.

Measured end-to-end:

  slice --entry Color --language java   --reverse   2 nodes -> 5
  slice --entry Color --language csharp --reverse   1 node  -> 4

Both languages ship together because the change is one shape applied
twice, and because this is the last slice: KNOWN_HOLES is now EMPTY and
every parametrized cell is a hard lock again. The WI-duguk drain ran
8 holes -> 0 across four slices (rust, typescript, swift, java/csharp).

Two findings are recorded in the gate's own comment rather than only in
the changelog, because they generalize past this item:

  1. A container that already emits SOME member kind reads as healthy
     here while another kind is wholly invisible -- java and swift enums
     emitted their methods the whole time. The parity fixtures therefore
     declare enums with members ONLY, which is what makes these cells
     measure what they claim.

  2. These columns measure ONE ANALYZER IN ISOLATION via span nesting, so
     a green cell is NOT evidence that reverse-slice expansion works end
     to end -- the container's kind must also be in
     linkers.containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and in the slicer's own set.
     `protocol` was in neither, so swift read green here while the live
     slice was unmoved. Re-run the end-to-end repro; do not certify from
     this matrix.

The now-dead _ENUM_HOLE / _ABSTRACT_HOLE reason strings are deleted
rather than left as unreferenced module state; the history they carried
lives in the KNOWN_HOLES comment.

